WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.595
Windows lets you install apps from anywhere. But outside the Microsoft Store,

2
00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:07.790
it's hard to tell who made an app,

3
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.855
or whether the file is genuine.

4
00:00:10.205 --> 00:00:12.570
In a survey of 60 Windows users,

5
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:17.671
87% stop or research an app when they don't recognize the publisher.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.437
Windows already has strong protection,

7
00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:29.744
but its warnings rarely explain why,

8
00:00:29.744 --> 00:00:32.862
and no warning doesn't feel like proof of safety.

9
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:36.762
So careful people check signatures, compare hashes,

10
00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:40.520
or upload files to VirusTotal. Everyone else guesses.

11
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:45.018
People weren't asking whether a file was signed.

12
00:00:45.368 --> 00:00:46.687
They were asking who made it.

13
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.105
So I split trust into two questions. Is the developer verified,

14
00:00:53.105 --> 00:00:56.982
with an identity that follows them across apps and releases?

15
00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:00.091
And is this exact file signed, unchanged,

16
00:01:00.091 --> 00:01:03.497
and recognized? Security signals fill in the rest,

17
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:05.812
so signed never has to mean safe.

18
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.506
Instead of block or allow,

19
00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:15.938
I designed four states. A trusted app from a verified developer installs after a short countdown.

20
00:01:16.288 --> 00:01:24.223
A signed app from an unverified developer gets a plain explanation and a choice. An unsigned app gets the strongest warning,

21
00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:27.935
and a new version from a known developer sits in between.

22
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:32.348
A warning alone leaves people stuck,

23
00:01:32.348 --> 00:01:35.670
so the experience also points to the Microsoft Store.

24
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:40.828
It looks for the same app from the same publisher first. When there isn't one,

25
00:01:40.828 --> 00:01:43.047
AI suggests a verified alternative.

26
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:47.280
To test it on real installers,

27
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.241
I built a working Windows app with AI. It runs when a setup file starts,

28
00:01:52.241 --> 00:01:54.308
or from the right-click menu.

29
00:01:54.658 --> 00:01:56.639
Each scan combines six checks,

30
00:01:56.639 --> 00:02:00.800
from the digital signature to Windows Defender and VirusTotal,

31
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.707
in about two seconds, without admin rights.

32
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.358
The trust states went into a pilot in China through Microsoft PC Manager,

33
00:02:11.358 --> 00:02:15.695
with more than 300,000 trust dialog views across 20 apps.

34
00:02:16.045 --> 00:02:19.803
And the working app showed the whole model holding up on real files.

35
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.458
Trust isn't a badge. It's knowing who made something,

36
00:02:25.458 --> 00:02:27.018
and what you're about to run.
